  "research_id": "programmable-and-agentic-payments-2026-09-22",
  "title": "Programmable Money and AI-Agent Payments — 2026",
  "question": "When a stablecoin, smart contract or AI agent moves money, what actually happened in law, on the ledger, in bank money, in the books and when something fails?",
  "bluf": "A smart contract makes authorization and evidence machine-readable, but it does not collapse a payment into one event: ownership, the token, the redemption claim, bank cash, the accounting entry, correction and bankruptcy can each have a different authority. Four accounting treatments of USDC are in use today (Coinbase books it as a cash equivalent, Galaxy and Figma at fair value, others as a receivable or prepaid), and once GENIUS is in force coins from unapproved issuers can't count as cash. Circle earned $667.7 million of reserve income in Q2 and paid Coinbase $324.6 million of it. For AI-agent payments, “authorization” is really five separate things: who is legally bound, what the agent can reach, what it may buy, what the bank accepts, and whether the deal was done.",
  "evidence_cutoff": "2026-09-22",
